course: everyday
lesson: 238
title: Bites, stings and things you find on the ground
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some small creatures bite or sting. Mosquitoes come out in the evening; covering your arms and legs and using repellent keeps them off. In Australia, redback and funnel-web spiders hide in shoes and under outdoor toilet seats, so shake shoes and check seats.
explain: Things found on the ground can be dangerous even if they look harmless. A needle can carry blood diseases, a bottle may hold a chemical, a firework or flare may still explode, and a rusty nail can cause tetanus if it cuts you.
explain: The rule is simple: do not touch, tell a grown-up. If you find money or a wallet, give it to a grown-up so it can go to the police or its owner.
explain: When you collect nature treasures for school, take only a few, and only where it is allowed, because some parks and beaches forbid it.
explain: Example: a boy in Sydney finds a syringe in the park. He does not touch it, puts his bag next to it to mark the spot and runs to tell his mum, who calls the council to remove it safely.
fact: Cover your arms and legs and use repellent in the evening, because mosquito bites itch and in some places carry diseases.
fact: In Australia, shake out shoes and check outdoor toilet seats, because redback and funnel-web spiders hide there.
fact: Keep away from ant hills and never sit on them, because fire ants in parts of the USA and Australia sting many times.
fact: Never touch a needle or syringe you find; mark the spot and tell a grown-up, because needles can carry blood-borne diseases.
fact: Never open, smell or drink from a bottle of liquid found outdoors, because it may contain chemicals.
fact: Give money or a wallet you find to a grown-up, so it can be handed to the police or the owner.
fact: Do not pick up a sharp rusty nail with bare hands; tell a grown-up, and tell them if it cut you, because rusty cuts can cause tetanus (also called lockjaw, an illness that makes the muscles go stiff; a doctor can stop it).
fact: Walk around animal poo and never touch it, because it carries worms and germs.
fact: Never touch fireworks, cartridges or flares found on the ground; tell a grown-up, because they may still explode.
fact: Take only a few stones, shells and leaves, and only where it is allowed, because some parks and beaches forbid taking natural things.
exercise: When are mosquitoes most likely to bite, and what helps? | In the evening; cover arms and legs and use repellent | At noon; wear shorts and a cap so your skin can breathe | In the evening; wear perfume to confuse them | Only late at night, and there is nothing at all that you can do about it
exercise: Why should you avoid mosquito bites, not just because they itch? | In some places mosquitoes carry diseases | Their bites leave scars that stay for many years | They lay their tiny eggs under your skin at night | They make you allergic to all other insects for life
exercise: A boy in Perth puts on shoes that were left on the back step overnight. What should he do first? | Shake them out to check for spiders | Stamp hard while putting them on | Spray water into them | Nothing, spiders never go into shoes
exercise: Why check under the seat of an outdoor toilet in Australia? | Redback spiders like to hide there | The seat is often wet from rain | Snakes nest inside the bowl | Toilet seats get too hot in the sun
exercise: You find a big ant hill in a park in Texas. What do you do? | Keep away and do not sit on it | Poke it with a stick to see the ants | Sit next to it to watch them work | Pour your drink on it to make them move
exercise: You find a syringe in the grass. What is right? | Do not touch it; mark the spot and tell a grown-up | Pick it up carefully by the end and put it in the nearest bin | Kick it under a bush so no one steps on it | Take it to school to show your teacher
exercise: Why are needles found on the ground dangerous? | They can carry blood-borne diseases | They are made of poisonous metal | They might explode if you press them | They attract snakes and spiders
exercise: You find a closed bottle of clear liquid by the path. What do you do? | Leave it; do not open, smell or drink from it | Smell it carefully to check if it is just water | Drink a little if you are very thirsty | Open it and pour it all out on the grass so nobody drinks it
exercise: You find a wallet with money on the pavement. What should you do? | Give it to a grown-up to hand to the police or owner | Keep the money, because finders are keepers | Take the money out and leave the wallet where it was | Leave it there for someone else to find
exercise: You see a rusty nail with a sharp point on a building site path. What is right? | Do not pick it up bare-handed; tell a grown-up | Pick it up and keep it for your collection of old metal | Throw it into the bushes with your hand | Step on it hard to push it down into the ground
exercise: A rusty nail scratched your hand. Why tell a grown-up? | Rusty cuts can cause tetanus, so it may need a doctor | So that they can clean the nail for you | Because the nail belongs to someone who will want it back | There is no need at all if it stops bleeding quickly
exercise: There is dog poo on the path. What do you do? | Walk around it and never touch it | Poke it with a stick to see if it is fresh | Kick it off the path with your shoe | Cover it with leaves using your hands
exercise: You find a flare or a firework on the beach after a festival. What do you do? | Do not touch it; tell a grown-up | Light it to see if it still works | Throw it far into the sea | Take it home for next year
exercise: Your class is collecting shells for a project. What is right? | Take only a few, and only where it is allowed | Take a whole bag full from every beach | Take shells with animals still living in them | It is always fine to take anything from nature
test: A girl in Brisbane goes to the outdoor toilet at her grandparents' farm. What should she do? | Check under the seat for spiders first | Sit down quickly so the spiders run away | Turn off the light so spiders cannot see | Nothing, spiders never come near toilets
test: Which is the best rule for strange things found on the ground? | Do not touch it; tell a grown-up | Pick it up if it looks clean | Smell it first to see what it is | Kick it away so nobody else finds it
test: Why might a firework lying in the grass be dangerous days later? | It may still explode | Fireworks turn into poison after use | It will attract wasps and bees | Fireworks melt in the sun and burn
test: A boy finds £20 in the street. What is honest? | Give it to a grown-up so it can go to the owner or police | Spend it, because no one saw him pick it up | Share it out fairly with his friends | Leave it, because money on the ground is dirty and full of germs
test: Why must you never sit on an ant hill in Florida? | Fire ants can sting many times | Ant hills are always full of snakes | You might crush the queen ant | Ants carry the flu
test: A park sign in New Zealand says «Do not remove rocks or plants». What should you do? | Leave the stones there and take a photo instead | Take just one big rock, because one does not count | Take them, as long as nobody is watching | Take them if they are for a school project about rocks
test: Your friend wants to sniff an old bottle found in the woods. What do you say? | No, it may contain a dangerous chemical | Yes, smelling it is safe if you do not drink | Yes, if it has a label on it | Only if it smells like juice
test: Why wash your hands after playing where dogs have been? | Animal poo can carry worms and germs | Dogs leave pollen on the grass | Grass makes your hands sticky | Dogs drop poisonous hairs
test: found a syringe in the grass near the swings | Don't touch it, tell a grown-up | Pick it up n bin it | Kick it into the bushes so no one steps on it | Take it home to show mum
test: found a wallet with $50 in it lol finders keepers | No, give it to a grown-up to return it | Yes, finders keepers | Keep the money, bin the wallet | Spend half n give back the other half, that's fair
test: stood on a rusty nail, bit of blood but it's fine | Tell a grown-up, rusty cuts can cause tetanus | Just keep playing, it'll stop bleeding | Put the nail back where it was | Pull your sock up over it n forget about it
test: sydney, putting my shoes on from the porch, any tips | Shake them out first, spiders can hide inside | Put them on fast before anything can get in them | Stamp hard while putting them on | Wear them without socks so you can feel if anything's in there
explain: A boy in Texas picked up a "dead" snake on a trail; it was alive, bit him, and he was rushed to hospital for antivenom.
explain: A girl in the UK picked up a shiny thing on the beach that turned out to be a sharp piece of metal; she cut her hand deeply and needed stitches.
fact: Snakes can bite even when they look dead, so you leave every snake alone.
fact: If someone is stung and then finds it hard to breathe, tell a grown-up at once and call the emergency number, because some people react badly to stings.
fact: Sharp objects, needles and strange things on the ground cause cuts and infections; you tell an adult instead of picking them up.
test: there's a snake on the path, looks dead | Leave it and walk well around it | Pick it up to check if it's really dead | Poke it with a long stick to see if it moves | Step over it quickly n keep going
test: Why should Tom not pick up the shiny metal thing he found on the beach? | It might be sharp or dangerous | It might rust all over his hands | It is bad luck to keep things from the beach | It is too heavy to carry all the way home
test: bee stung me and i'm finding it hard to breathe | Tell an adult now and call the emergency number | Lie down n have a sleep till it passes | Drink lots of water | Scratch the sting out hard with ur nails
test: did u hear an Aussie kid put his shoe on and a spider in it bit him and he went to hospital? real lesson? | Shake out shoes before putting them on | Spiders only live in trees | Only ever wear sandals in Australia | Spiders can't bite through socks
test: apparently a boy found a wallet with 500 dollars in the park and kept it and got arrested. what's the true lesson? | Give found money or a wallet to an adult | Keep it if nobody saw you pick it up | Spend it quickly before someone asks | Leave it where it is forever
test: my mate says if u step on a rusty nail u get lockjaw and can never open ur mouth again. probably exaggerated? | Never again is stretched, but tell an adult about rusty cuts | Totally true for every nail | Rust is harmless so ignore it | Only new nails are dangerous
exam: Identify the appropriate course of action upon finding a needle or syringe outdoors. | It is not touched; the spot is marked and an adult told | It is picked up with care and placed in the nearest bin | It is kicked into the grass | It is taken home in a pocket in order to show one's parents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a snake that appears dead? | It may still bite and is left alone | It cannot bite and may be handled | It may be picked up safely by the tail | It is safe once it stops moving
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice upon finding a cartridge or flare on the ground. | It is not touched, as it may explode, and an adult is told | It is collected and kept as a souvenir | It is thrown into a fire in order to dispose of it safely | It is opened to inspect it
exam: Ascertain the reason for which one does not sit upon ant hills in parts of the USA and Australia. | Fire ants there may sting many times | Ants there are poisonous to eat | The hills are protected by law | The hills are made of hot sand
